      "syllabus": "Course Objectives:\nTo learn\n• Methods of solving the differential equations of first and higher order.\n• Concept, properties of Laplace transforms.\n• Solving ordinary differential equations using Laplace transform techniques.\n• The physical quantities involved in the engineering field related to vector-valued functions\n• The basic properties of vector-valued functions and their applications to line, surface and volume\nintegrals\n\nUNIT-I:First Order Ordinary Differential Equations 8 Hrs\n\nExact differential equations — Equations form: Homogeneous form and yf(xy)dx + xg(xy)dy = OReducible to\nexact differential equations — linear and Bernoulli’s equations. Applications: Newton’s law of cooling — Law of\nnatural growth and decay.\n\nUNIT-II:Ordinary Differential Equations of Higher Order 10 Hrs\n\nHigher order linear differential equations with constant coefficients: Non-Homogeneous terms of the type\nV(x): é, sinsin ax, cos cos ax, polynomials in x, e” V(x) and xV(x) — Method of variation of parameters.\n\nUNIT-III:Laplace Transforms 10 Hrs\nLaplace Transforms: Laplace Transform of standard functions — First shifting theorem — Second shifting\ntheorem-Laplace transforms of functions multiplied by 't' and divided by 't' — Evaluation of integrals by Laplace\ntransforms — Inverse Laplace transform by different methods, convolution theorem (without proof).\nApplications: solving Initial value problems by Laplace Transform method.\n\nUNIT-IV:Vector Differentiation 10 Hrs\n\nVector point functions and scalar point functions — Gradient — Divergence and Curl — Directional derivatives —\nAngle between two surfaces — Scalar potential functions — Solenoidal and Irrotational vectors.\n\nUNIT-V:Vector Integration 10 Hrs\n\nLine, Surface and Volume Integrals. Theorems of Green, Gauss and Stokes (without proofs) and their\napplications.",
