      "code": "2599202",
      "name": "Engineering Physics",
      "l": 3,
      "t": 0,
      "p": 0,
      "credits": 3,
      "syllabus": "Course Objectives:\n\n• To understand fundamental concepts of quantum mechanics and their applications in solids and\nnanomaterials.\n\n• To give students a clear understanding of how semiconductors and nanomaterials work, how they are\nmade, and how they are used in modern technology\n\n• To introduce quantum computing principles, quantum gates, and basic quantum algorithms.\n\n• To learn the properties and applications of magnetic and dielectric materials.\n\n• To explore the working and applications of lasers and fibre optics in modern technology.\n\nUNIT-I: 12Hrs\n\nQuantum Mechanics: Introduction, de-Broglie hypothesis, Davisson-Germer Experiment Heisenberg\nuncertainty principle, physical significance of wave function, postulates of quantum mechanics (Elementary\ntreatment): operators in quantum mechanics, eigen values and eigen functions, expectation value;\nSchrédinger’s time independent wave equation, particle ina 1D box.\n\nBloch’s theorem (Elementary treatment), Kronig-Penney model (Elementary treatment): E-K diagram, effective\nmass of electron (Elementary treatment), formation of energy bands, classification of solids.\n\nUNIT-II: 12 Hrs\n\nSemiconductor Physics: Intrinsic and extrinsic semiconductors, variation of Fermi energy level with\ntemperature and doping concentration (Elementary treatment), direct and indirect band gap semiconductors,\nHall Effect, construction, principle, V-I characteristics and applications of P-N Junction diode, LED and solar\ncell.\n\nNanotechnology: Introduction to nanoscience and nanotechnology, Classification of nanomaterials:\n0D,1D,2D and 3D, surface to volume ratio, quantum confinement, synthesis methods: Ball milling, sol-gel\nmethod, Characterization Techniques: X-ray diffraction (XRD) and Scanning Electron Microscopy (SEM),\napplications of nanomaterials.\n\nUNIT-IIl: 12Hrs\n\nQuantum Computing: Introduction, linear algebra for quantum computation(Elementary treatment), Dirac's\nBra and Ket notation and their properties, superposition, entanglement, Hilbert space, Bloch's sphere, concept\nof quantum computer: classical bits, Qubits, multiple Qubit system, quantum computing system for information\nprocessing (Elementary treatment), evolution of quantum systems, quantum measurements, quantum gates\n(Elementary treatment): Pauli, Hadamard, and CNOT gates, challenges and advantages of quantum\ncomputing over classical computation.\n\nUNIT-IV: 12 Hrs\n\nDielectric materials: Introduction to dielectric materials, types of polarization: electronic, ionic (Quantitative)\n& orientation (Elementary treatment); ferroelectric, piezoelectric, pyroelectric materials and their properties\nand applications\n\nMagnetic materials: Introduction to magnetic materials, origin of magnetic moment- classification of\nmagnetic materials: dia, para, ferro, anti-ferro and ferri magnetic materials. Weiss domain theory of\nferromagnetism, Hysteresis and B-H curve, soft and hard magnetic materials, applications.\n\nUNIT-V: 12Hrs\nLaser: Introduction to laser, characteristics of laser, Einstein coefficients and their relations, metastable state,\npopulation inversion, pumping, lasing action, He-Ne laser, Nd-YAG Laser, semiconductor diode laser,\n\napplications of Lasers.\nFibre Optics: Introduction to fibre optics, total internal reflection, Physical components of optical fibre,\n\nacceptance angle, numerical aperture, classification of optical fibres (based on refractive index profile), losses\nin optical fibre, applications: optical fibre for communication system, sensor for structural health monitoring.",
      "textbooks": "1. M.N Avadhanulu, P.G Kshirsagar, T VS Arun Murthy, A Textbook of Engineering Physics, S\nChand,2019.\n2. Shatendra Sharma and Jyotsna Sharma, Engineering Physics,Pearson India Education Services Pvt.\nLtd,2018.\n\n3. Michael A. Nielsen & Isaac L. Chuang, Quantum Computation and Quantum Information, Cambridge\nUniversity Press.\n\nReferences Books:\n1. Charles Kittel, Introduction to Solid State Physics, John Wiley & Sons, Inc.\n2. Md. N. Khan, S. Panigrahi,Principles of Engineering Physics 1 & 2,Cambridge University Press, 2016\n3. Prof. A. K. Katiyar, Dr. C. K. Pandey, Engineering Physics: Theory and Practical, Wiley India Pvt. Ltd.,\nIndia,2017.\n4. Halliday, Resnick and Walker, Fundamentals of Physics, John Wiley &Sons,11th Edition, 2018.",
      "referenceBooks": "",
      "outcomes": "CO-1. Apply quantum mechanical principles to explain particle behaviour and energy band formation in\nsolids\n\nCO-2. Able to explain the principles, types, fabrication methods, and applications of semiconductors and\nnanomaterials in simple terms.\n\nCO-3. Understand quantum computing concepts, use quantum gates, and explain basic quantum\nalgorithms.\n\nCO-4. Classify magnetic and dielectric materials and explain their properties, synthesis, and applications.\n\nCO-5. Explain the principles of lasers and fibre optics and their applications in communication and sensing."
