      "syllabus": "Course Objectives:\n• To introduce programming languages, problem-solving methods, algorithms, flowcharts, and the\nbasics of Python.\n• To understand Python data types, variables, operators, and input/output operations.\n• To apply control statements and arrays for solving computational problems.\n• To develop modular programs using functions, recursion, and scope of variables.\n• To implement exception handling and file operations for robust Python applications.\n\nUNIT -I 12 Hrs\n\nINTRODUCTION TO PROGRAMMING LANGUAGES: Computer definition, Computer system, Programming\nlanguages: Types, Uses. Program development steps, Algorithm, Flowchart, Solving simple problems.\nINTRODUCTION TO PYTHON: History of Python, Features of Python, Flavors of Python, Execution of a\nPython Program, Python Virtual Machine, Viewing the byte code, Comparisons between C and Python,\nComments in Python, Docstrings.\n\nUNIT - II 12 Hrs\n\nDATATYPES IN PYTHON: Built-in data types: None, Numeric and Boolean data types, Sequences in Python:\nstrings, bytes, bytearrays, lists, tuples, sets, frozensets, dictionaries. User-defined data types, constants in\nPython, identifiers and reserved words, and naming conventions in Python.\n\nVARIABLES AND OPERATORS: Understanding Python variables, multiple variable declarations; Operators\nin Python - Arithmetic operators, Assignment operators, Relational Operators, Logical operators, Boolean\nOperators, Bitwise operators, Membership operators, Identity operators. Operator Precedence and\nAssociativity.\n\nInput Output Statements: Output statements, Input Statements and Command Line Arguments, A word on\nIndentation.\n\nUNIT - Ill 12. Hrs\n\nCONTROL STATEMENTS: The if Statement, The if else statements, The if...elif...else statements, while\nloop, for loop, Infinite loop, Nested Loops, else suite, break, continue, pass statement, assert statement.\nARRAYS: Introduction to Arrays, Creating an Array, Types of arrays, Importing the array Module and NumPy,\nIndexing and Slicing on numpyArrays, Mathematical Operations on Arrays, Comparison of Arrays.\n\nUNIT -IV 12 Hrs\n\nFUNCTIONS: Introduction, Defining a Function, calling a function, Formal and Actual Arguments, Positional\nArguments, keyword Arguments, Default Arguments, variable length arguments, local and global variables,\nglobal keyword, nesting of functions and Recursion.\n\nUNIT-V 12. Hrs\n\nEXCEPTIONS IN PYTHON: Errors in Python Program - Compile-Time Errors, Runtime Errors, Logical Errors;\nExceptions, Exception Handling, Types of Exceptions, and Keywords in Exception Handling.\n\nFILES IN PYTHON: Types of Files in Python, Opening a File, Closing a File, Random File accessing\nmethods- seek() and tell().",
      "textbooks": "1. R. Nageswara Rao, “Core Python Programming”, Dreamtech\n2. Fundamentals of Python First Programs, Kenneth A. Lambert, 2\"4 Edition, Cengage.",
      "referenceBooks": "1. Allen B. Downey, “Think Python: How to Think like a Computer Scientist”, 2nd edition, Updated for\nPython 3, Shroff/O‘Reilly Publishers, 2016.\n2. Core Python Programming, W.Chun, Pearson.",
      "outcomes": "After completion of the Course, the student should be able to\n\nCO-1. Understand problem-solving strategies, algorithms, flowcharts, and the basics of Python\nprogramming.\n\nCO-2. Apply Python data types, variables, operators, and I/O operations to solve simple problems.\n\nCO-3. Use control structures and arrays to implement Python programs.\n\nCO-4. Develop modular solutions using functions, recursion, and scope management.\n\nCO-5. Implement exception handling and file operations for building reliable Python applications."
