      "syllabus": "Course Objectives:\n\n• Exploring knowledge of basic data structures and their implementations.\n\n• To understand tree data structures, their operations, and applications with emphasis on BSTs, AVL\ntrees, and Red-Black trees.\n\n• Understand multi-way search trees and also explain the need for searching algorithms.\n\n• To learn graph structures, their representations, traversals, and applications, and to understand\nefficient sorting techniques, including Radix, Heap, Merge, and Quick sort.\n\n• To understand hashing concepts, hash functions, and collision resolution techniques for efficient data\nstorage and retrieval.\n\nUNIT -I 12 Hrs\n\nIntroduction to Data Structures: Basic Terminology, Classification of Data Structures, Operations on Data\nStructures, and Abstract Data Types.\n\nLinear list — Introduction, singly linked list, Circular Single Linked Lists, Stacks- Operations, Stack algorithm,\nStack ADT, Stack applications, Queues- operations, Queue Algorithm, Queue ADT, and Queue Applications.\n\nUNIT - Il 12 Hrs\n\nTrees: Introduction, Types of Trees, creating a Binary Tree from a General Tree, traversing a Binary Tree,\nBinary Search Trees (BST), BST Operations - Searching, Insertion, and Deletion, BST ADT, BST\nApplications, AVL Trees, Red—Black Trees.\n\nUNIT - Ill 12 Hrs\n\nMulti-way Search Trees: Introduction, B Trees, B Trees ADT, B+ Trees, Heaps: Binary Heaps, Fibonacci\nHeaps, Applications.\nSearching: Introduction, Interpolation Search, Jump Search.\n\nUNIT -IV 12 Hrs\n\nGraphs: Introduction, Directed Graphs, Representation of Graphs, Graph Traversal Algorithms, Graph ADT,\nApplications of Graphs.\nSorting: Radix Sort, Heap Sort, Merge Sort. Quick Sort.\n\nUNIT-V 12. Hrs\nHashing and Collision: Introduction, Hash Tables, Hash Functions, Different Hash Functions: Division\nMethod, Multiplication Method, Mid-square Method.\n\nCollisions: Introduction, Collision Resolution by Chaining, Open addressing: Linear Probing, Quadratic\n\nProbing.",
