      "syllabus": "Course Objectives:\n\n• To provide hands-on experience in studying the electrical, optical, and magnetic properties of various\nmaterials.\n\n• To understand the working principles and characteristics of semiconductor devices like PN junction\ndiodes, LEDs, and solar cells.\n\n• To explore advanced concepts in modern physics through experiments on lasers, the photoelectric\neffect, and optical fibers.\n\n• To develop experimental skills for measuring fundamental constants and material parameters.\n\n• Toimprove abilities in scientific data collection, result analysis, and technical reporting.\n\nList of experiments:\n\n1. Determination of the Energy gap of a semiconductor.\n2. |-V Characteristics of P-N junction diode.\n3. B-H curve of a ferromagnetic material.\n4. (a) V-I Characteristics of LED diode.\n(b) V-I Characteristics of a solar cell.\n5. Determination of the wavelength of a laser using a diffraction grating.\n6. Determination of Planck’s constant using the photoelectric effect.\n7. Determination of the magnetic moment of a bar magnet and the horizontal Earth's magnetic field.\n8. (a) Determine the Numerical Aperture of an Optical Fiber.\n(b) Determine the bending losses of an Optical Fiber.\n9. Determination of the dielectric constant of a given material.\n10. Determination of Hall coefficient and carrier concentration of a given semiconductor.\n\nNote: Any eight (08) experiments are to be performed.\nSelf-Practice Programs:\n\n1. Determination of Hall coefficient and carrier concentration of a given semiconductor.\n2. Determination of the dielectric constant of a given material.",
