      "credits": 1,
      "syllabus": "Course Objectives:\n\n• To familiarize students with Python environment setup, syntax, operators, and basic input/output\nprograms.\n\n• To develop problem-solving skills using decision-making, control structures, and iterative constructs.\n\n• To implement and manipulate Python data structures such as lists, tuples, dictionaries, and arrays\n(including NumPy).\n\n• To design modular programs using user-defined functions and recursion.\n\n• Toimplement file handling, exception handling, and command-line arguments for robust applications.\n\nList of Experiments:\n\nWeek — 1:\n\n1. Demonstrate the installation of Python for windows\n\n2. Demonstrate the execution of Python program in Jupyter notebook and IDLE\n\n3. Demonstrate the importing of NumPy, pandas, matplotlib and mention briefly about their purpose.\n4. Write a Python program to assign a value to a variable and print the value of the variable.\n\nWeek -— 2:\n\n5. Write a Python program to assign different values to a single variable and print the value of the variable.\n6. Write a Python program to assign multiple values to multiple variables and print the value of the variable.\n7. Write a python program for printing values using format() method, % operator and format specifier.\n\n8. Write a Python program to read two numbers from the user and print their sum and difference.\n\nWeek -— 3:\n\n9. Write a Python program implementing a simple calculator using all arithmetic operators.\n\n10. Write a Python program illustrating the relation between the given two numbers using relational\noperators.\n\n11. Write a Python program illustrating all logical operators.\n\n12. Write a Python program illustrating all bitwise operators.\n\nWeek - 4:\n13. Write a Python program to perform the swapping of the given two numbers:\ni) Using a temporary variable __ ii) Without using a temporary variable\n14. Write a Python program to evaluate the following expressions:\ni) r#at+b*c-d/e _ ii) r//((atb) * ((c- d)/e))\n15. Write a Python program to calculate Simple Interest: S/ = (P x T x R) /100\n\nWeek -— 5:\n16. Write a Python program to check whether the given number is positive or negative using a simple if\nstatement.\n\n17. Write a Python program to find the largest of the given three numbers using a selection statement.\n18. Write a Python program to display the corresponding day of a given number in a week.\n19. Write a Python program to check whether the given year is a leap year or not.\n\nWeek - 6:\n\n20. Write a Python program to print natural numbers up to a given range.\n\n21. Write a Python program to print the factors of a given number.\n\n22. Write a Python program to check whether the given number is Armstrong or not.\n\nWeek - 7:\n23. Write a Python program to print numbers that are divisible by a given number up to a given range using\nunconditional statements.\n\n24. Write a Python program to create a list and print all elements of the list using the list method.\n\nWeek - 8:\n\n25. Write a Python program demonstrating the following operations on a tuple:\ni) | Create a tuple and copy the contents of the list.\nii) Delete elements from the tuple after displaying the contents.\n\n26. Write a Python program demonstrating the following operations on a dictionary:\ni) | Create a dictionary and display the contents.\nii) Create a dictionary having multiple values for the same key.\niii) | Concatenate two lists and convert them into a dictionary\n\nWeek - 9:\n27. Write a Python program to create a single-dimensional array and display its contents.\n28. Write a Python program implementing the following operations on arrays using the array module:\ni) Print the first n elements\nii) Print the elements with the given stride value\niii) Print the elements in reverse order.\niv) Copy the contents into another array.\n\nWeek — 10:\n29. Write a Python program implementing the following operations on arrays using the NumPy module:\ni) | Create a 2x3 array and add elements\nii) Convert the dimensions of the array into 3x2.\niii) Convert the dimensions of the array into 1x6.\niv) Copy the contents into another array.\n30. Write a Python program to perform Matrix addition and Matrix multiplication.\n\nWeek - 11:\n31. Write a Python program to find the sum of n natural numbers using user-defined functions.\n32. Write a Python program having a function that takes a list and returns a sorted list.\n\nWeek — 12:\n\n33. Write a Python program to print the factorial of a given number using recursive function.\n34. Write a Python program to display the Fibonacci series up to a given range using functions:\ni) Without using recursion ii) Using recursion\n\nWeek — 13:\n35. Write a Python program illustrating the occurrence of an exception.\n36. Write a Python program to create a new file and add the given text to the file.\n\nWeek — 14:\n\n37. Write a Python program demonstrating command-line arguments.\n\n38. Write a Python program to add the contents into an existing file and display the contents of a file\nfrom the specified position.\n\nSelf-Practice Programs:\n\n1. Learn PEP-8 Coding Programs.\n2. Introduction to OOP Concepts",
      "textbooks": "1. R.Nageswara Rao, “Core Python Programming”, Dreamtech\n2. Fundamentals of Python First Programs, Kenneth A. Lambert, 2nd Edition, Cengage.",
      "referenceBooks": "1. Allen B. Downey, “Think Python: How to Think like a Computer Scientist’, 2nd edition, Updated for\nPython 3, Shroff/O‘Reilly Publishers, 2016.\n2. Core Python Programming, W.Chun, Pearson.",
      "outcomes": "After completion of the Course, the student should be able to\n\nCO-1. Apply Python syntax, operators, and I/O features to write and execute programs.\nCO-2. Apply operators, control statements, and loops to solve computational problems.\n\nCO-3. Use data structures like lists, tuples, dictionaries, and arrays to develop Python programs.\nCO-4. Develop modular solutions using functions and recursion.\nCo-5. Implement file operations, exception handling, and command-line arguments in Python."
