      "syllabus": "Course Objectives:\n\n• To introduce number systems, digital logic circuits, and basic computer organization.\n\n• To understand register transfer language, micro-operations, and basic computer organization and\ndesign.\n\n• To learn microprogrammed control and CPU organization including instruction formats, addressing\nmodes, and program control.\n\n• To explore input-output organization and memory hierarchy including cache and associative memory.\n\n• To understand the concepts of pipelines, vector processing, multiprocessors, and RISC/CISC\narchitectures.\n\nUNIT -I (The Unit is being dealt only for basic information & should be treated 12 Hrs\nas elementary)\n\nData Representation: Number Systems, Complements, Arithmetic Addition, Arithmetic Subtraction, Overflow.\nDigital Logic Circuits: Logic Gates, Boolean Algebra, Map Simplification, Combinational Circuits: Half Adder,\nFull Adder; Flip-Flops — SR, D, JK and T; Sequential Circuits: Flip-Flop Input Equations, State Table and State\nDiagram.\n\nDigital Components-Decoders, Multiplexers, Registers, Binary Counters. Digital Computers -Block diagram\nof Digital Computer, Definition of Computer Organization, Computer Design and Computer Architecture.\n\nUNIT - Il 12 Hrs\n\nRegister Transfer Language and Micro Operations: Register Transfer language, Register Transfer, Bus\nand Memory Transfers, Arithmetic Micro Operations, Logic Micro Operations, Shift Micro Operations,\nArithmetic Logic Shift Unit.\n\nBasic Computer Organization and Design: Instruction codes, Computer Registers Computer instructions,\nTiming and Control, Instruction Cycle, Memory Reference Instructions, Input — Output and Interrupt.\n\nUNIT - Ill 12 Hrs\n\nMicroprogrammed Control: Control memory, Address sequencing, Micro program example, Design of\nControl Unit.\n\nCentral Processing Unit: General Register Organization, Instruction Formats, Addressing modes, Data\nTransfer and Manipulation, Program Control.\n\nUNIT -IV 12 Hrs\n\nInput-Output Organization: Input-Output Interface, Asynchronous Data Transfer, Modes of Transfer, Priority\n\nInterrupt Direct memory Access.\n\nMemory Organization: Memory Hierarchy, Main Memory, Auxiliary memory, Associate Memory, Cache\n\nMemory.\n\nUNIT-V 12 Hrs\nPipeline and Vector Processing: Parallel Processing, Pipelining, Arithmetic Pipeline, Instruction Pipeline.\n\nMulti Processors: Characteristics of Multiprocessors, Interconnection Structures.\nCharacteristics of RISC and CISC.",
      "textbooks": "1. Computer System Architecture — M. Morris Mano, Third Edition, Pearson/PHI.",
      "referenceBooks": "1. Computer Organization — Carl Hamacher, ZvonksVranesic, SafeaZaky, 5th Edition, McGraw-Hill.\n2. Computer Organization and Architecture — William Stallings, Sixth Edition, Pearson/PHI.\n3. Structured Computer Organization — Andrew S. Tanenbaum, 4th Edition, PHI/Pearson.",
      "outcomes": "After completion of the Course, the student should be able to\n\nCO-1. Explain number systems, digital logic, flip-flops, and basic digital computer structure.\n\nCO-2. Apply register transfer language and micro-operations in designing simple computer instructions.\n\nCO-3. Demonstrate microprogrammed control, CPU organization, instruction formats, and addressing\nmodes.\n\nCO-4. Analyze I/O organization and memory hierarchy, including cache and associative memory.\n\nCO-5. Compare pipelining, multiprocessors, and RISC/CISC architectures."
