      "syllabus": "Course Objectives:\n• To introduce ER data model and Relational data model\n• To design database schema for a given application and apply normalization.\n• To gain knowledge of SQL commands for data definition and data manipulation\n• To understand the basics of Querying\n• To develop solutions for database applications using procedures, cursors and triggers\n\nList of Experiments\n\n1. Concept design with E-R Model\n\n2. Relational Model\n\n3. Normalization\n\n4. Practicing DDL commands\n\n5. Practicing DML commands\n\n6. A. Querying (using ANY, ALL, UNION, INTERSECT, JOIN, Constraints etc.)\nB. Nested, Correlated Subqueries\n\n7. Queries using Aggregate functions, GROUP BY, HAVING, and Creation and dropping of Views.\n\n8. Usage of Cursors\n\n9. Triggers (Creation of insert trigger, delete trigger, update trigger)\n\n10. Procedures\n\nSelf-Practice Programs:\n\n1.Enhanced ER (EER) modeling with specialization, generalization, and aggregation,\n\nMapping complex real-world scenarios to ER diagrams\n2.Normalization in NoSQL databases, Automated functional dependency discovery tools\n3.Database security concepts: roles, privileges, and fine-grained access control,Materialized views\n4.Recursive queries using WITH RECURSIVE, Event-driven programming with triggers\n5.Procedures with IN, OUT, and INOUT parameters, Parameterized cursors",
