      "syllabus": "Course Objectives:\n• To explain the basic concepts, types, and process of machine learning\n• To elaborate feature selection, dimensionality reduction algorithms and Classification algorithms\n• To introduce regression algorithms, Bayes classification\n• To enable students to explore unsupervised learning algorithms\n• Tounderstand the fundamentals of Perceptron Learning and ANN\n\nUNIT -I 12 Hrs\n\nIntroduction to Machine Learning: Types of Human learning, machine learning process, Well-posed\nlearning problem, Types of machine learning and comparison, applications of machine learning.\n\nModelling and Evaluation: selecting a model, predictive and descriptive models, supervised learning model\ntraining, cross-validation and boot strapping, lazy vs eager learner, interpreting the model- underfitting,\noverfitting, bias-variance trade-off. Parameter for evaluating performance of classification, regression, and\nclustering model. Improving the performance of a model.\n\nUNIT - II 12. Hrs\n\nFeature Engineering: Feature transformation - feature construction, feature extraction by PCA, SVD, LDA.\nFeature subset selection -feature relevancyand redundancy measures. Feature selection process and\napproaches.\n\nSupervised Learning - Classification: Introduction, Classification model, learning steps, Classification\nalgorithms: k-Nearest Neighbor (KNN), Decision tree, RandomForest, Support vector machines, Kernel trick.\n\nUNIT - Ill 12. Hrs\n\nRegression: Introduction of regression, Regression algorithms: Simple linear regression, Multiple linear\nregression, Polynomial regression model, Logistic regression, Maximum likelihood estimation.\n\nBayesian Learning: Bayes Theorem, Baye’s Theorem and ConceptLearning, Bayes Optimal classifier,\nNaive Bayes classifier, Bayesian belief network.\n\nUNIT -IV 12 Hrs\nUnsupervised Learning: Introduction to unsupervised learning, Unsupervised vs supervised learning,\nApplication of unsupervised learning, Clustering and its types, Partitioning method: k-Means and K- Medoids,\nHierarchical clustering, Density-based methods - DBSCAN\n\nUNIT-V 12. Hrs\n\nArtificial Neural Network: Biological neuron, Artificial neuron, Activation functions, neural network\narchitecture, perceptron, learning process in ANN, Back propagation.",
      "textbooks": "1. Saikat Dutt, S. Chandramouli, Das — Machine Learning, First Edition, Pearson, 2018.\n2. MN Murty, Anathanarayana V S — Machine Learning, First Edition, University Press, 2024\n3. Tom M Mitchell, —Machine Learning, First Edition, McGraw Hill Education, 2013.",
      "referenceBooks": "1. Stephen Marsland, —Machine Learning — An Algorithmic Perspective, Second Edition, 2014.\n2. Chapman and Hall/CRC Machine Learning and Pattern Recognition Series, 2014.",
      "outcomes": "After completion of the Course, the student will be able to\n\nCO-1. Choose an appropriate machine learning model and evaluate a model for a given task\n\nCO-2. Apply feature selection methods, dimensionality reduction methods and classification techniques\nCO-3. Implement regression algorithms and Bayes classification\n\nCO-4. Implement unsupervised machine learning algorithms\n\nCO-5. Design, train and evaluate Perceptron models"
