      "syllabus": "Course Objectives:\n• To understand the basic concepts and functionalities of operating systems.\n• To apply UNIX/Linux system calls for process, file, and memory management.\n• To simulate CPU scheduling and understand its effect on performance.\n• Toimplement synchronization problems using semaphores and IPC mechanisms.\n• To analyze and simulate memory management techniques and page replacement policies.\n\nList of Experiments\n\n1. Write programs using the I/O system calls of UNIX/LINUX operating system (open, read, write, close,\nIseek, stat, fork, exit) .\n2. Write C programs to simulate the following CPU Scheduling algorithms\na) FCFS b) SJF .\n3. Write C programs to simulate the following CPU Scheduling algorithms\na)Round Robin b) priority .\n4. Write a C program to implement the Producer — Consumer problem using semaphores\nusing UNIX/LINUX system calls.\n5. Write C programs to illustrate the following IPC mechanisms\na) Pipes b) FIFOs.\n6. Write C programs to illustrate the following IPC mechanisms\na) Message Queues b) Shared Memory.\n7. Write a C program to simulate Bankers Algorithm for Deadlock Avoidance.\n8. Write C programs to simulate the following memory management techniques\na) Paging b) Segmentation .\n9. Write C programs to simulate Page replacement policy FIFO.\n10. Write C programs to simulate Page replacement policy LRU.\n\nSelf-Practice Programs:\n\n1. Scheduling simulation using Gantt charts,\n2. Deadlock detection algorithm implementation,\n3. Demand paging strategies.",
